          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Montesquieu</strong><br>\nCharles-Louis de Secondat, known as Montesquieu, was born into a noble family in 1689 in the La Brède region of France. From a young age, he received a classical education, studying the sciences, history, and law. This education laid the foundation for his future work in political theory and philosophy. Montesquieu traveled extensively throughout Europe, observing different forms of government.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Montesquieu's Legal Career</strong><br>\nAfter completing his education, Montesquieu began a career in law. He became a counselor in the Parliament of Bordeaux in 1714 and later its president. His experience in the legal system provided him with firsthand insights into the operations of French governance. His observations during this time would heavily influence his later writings on political theory.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Persian Letters</strong><br>\nMontesquieu's first major work, \"The Persian Letters,\" was published anonymously in 1721. This satirical novel explored the flaws of European culture and the French government through the eyes of two Persian travelers. The book's clever critique of society and politics earned Montesquieu a reputation as a sharp and insightful thinker. It also provided a cover for discussing controversial ideas without direct attribution.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
