          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Montesquieu's Research Method</strong><br>\nMontesquieu believed in the empirical approach to understanding political systems, meaning he relied on observation and experience as evidence. He devoted himself to extensive research, comparing different governments to deduce general principles about how they functioned. His methods were revolutionary for the time and paved the way for modern sociological and political research.</p>\n\n<p><strong>On the Spirit of Laws</strong><br>\nIn 1748, Montesquieu published \"On the Spirit of Laws,\" his most famous work. This extensive treatise outlined his theory that political systems should be suited to the climate, population, economy, and traditions of their countries. He argued for a separation of powers within government to prevent any one branch from becoming too powerful.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Separation of Powers</strong><br>\nMontesquieu is best known for his advocacy of the separation of powers within a government. He believed that dividing governmental powers among executive, legislative, and judicial branches would protect political liberty and prevent despotism. His ideas strongly influenced the framers of the United States Constitution.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
